	In our game (we are the ones who did the grenade code), about
one soldier in five carries two grenades, and they know how to use them.
They arm them and throw them at you, pick up unarmed grenades on the
ground, and steer clear of armed grenades on the ground.  It is always
annoying to get the message "An armed grenade lands at your feet."
as it means that it is time to run away.

Specifics: A soldier decides to throw a grenade at you whenever he has
straight line of sight to you (like any other monster with a missle weapon).
He uses up the first turn to arm the grenade.  The next turn he throws
it at you.  If you have moved out of sight on the next turn, he has to
throw it away anyway, so he throws it to where you used to be standing.

( In the first version, if the soldier lost sight of you, he would change
his mind about throwing the grenade, and just put it back into his pack
or whatever.  4 turns later, the grenade would go off, and toast him and his
buddies.  It was a fun strategy. )

If you wake up a whole barracks, the next turn you will often find
4 or 5 grenades landing at your feet.  If only one grenade lands
at your feet, you have just enough time to pick it up and throw it back
at the sucker.  But while you're busy doing that, the soldier usually
throws the other grenade at you.  After a good fire battle, whole
sections of dungeon are ripped apart.

Soldiers avoid armed grenades:  the code for this was mostly there
already (i.e. tame dogs avoid cursed objects), but some modification
was necessary to avoid the whole blast radius.  If a soldier stupidly
throws a grenade into the middle of the barracks, the soldiers all try
to run away, but they get in each other's way, and many get hit anyway.

Soldiers pick up unarmed grenades:  Simple -- the code already exists
(Orcs pick up gold, etc).  This allows soldiers to pick up grenades 
from dead comrades and use them.

Dropping a live grenade behind you when running away is useful for slowing
down the soldiers (they will wait for the grenade to go off before
running across it), even if it doesn't actually cause any damage to
any of them.

In our version, hobgoblins occasionally get created with grenades
as well.  It's worth wasting a charge on a long-range wand to kill
them quickly to get the unused grenades.

--Rich

